1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,692
It's three in the morning and you're awake.

2
00:00:02,692 --> 00:00:04,528
Not because anything's wrong.

3
00:00:04,528 --> 00:00:08,873
Because your brain has decided, for no reason it's willing to explain,

4
00:00:08,873 --> 00:00:13,462
that now is the time to think about a conversation you had four years ago.

5
00:00:13,462 --> 00:00:14,931
You look at your phone.

6
00:00:14,931 --> 00:00:15,910
The blue light.

7
00:00:15,910 --> 00:00:16,705
The numbers.

8
00:00:16,705 --> 00:00:17,746
You put it down.

9
00:00:17,746 --> 00:00:19,092
You pick it up again.

10
00:00:19,092 --> 00:00:22,886
Here's the thing nobody tells you about three in the morning.

11
00:00:22,886 --> 00:00:24,232
You didn't invent it.

12
00:00:24,232 --> 00:00:27,965
That hour has been a problem for two hundred thousand years,

13
00:00:27,965 --> 00:00:31,759
and for almost all of that time, there was no phone to blame.

14
00:00:31,759 --> 00:00:33,717
The dark was the whole problem.

15
00:00:33,717 --> 00:00:35,553
And somebody had to solve it.

16
00:00:35,553 --> 00:00:40,693
The sun has been down for an hour, and Renn's family is still half a day from home.

17
00:00:40,693 --> 00:00:41,856
Renn is in front —

18
00:00:41,856 --> 00:00:42,957
of course he is —

19
00:00:42,957 --> 00:00:47,424
carrying the spear he will absolutely tell you he used heroically today.

20
00:00:47,424 --> 00:00:52,503
Behind him, Sena is walking with her hand on Tep's shoulder, because Tep is eight,

21
00:00:52,503 --> 00:00:56,970
and eight-year-olds get tired in a way that makes them walk into things.

22
00:00:56,970 --> 00:00:58,061
There's no moon.

23
00:00:58,061 --> 00:00:59,922
There's no path, not really.

24
00:00:59,922 --> 00:01:04,735
There's the sound of the lake behind them and the sound of their own feet,

25
00:01:04,735 --> 00:01:07,045
and that is genuinely all there is.

26
00:01:07,045 --> 00:01:09,612
Now, you know what happens in the dark.

27
00:01:09,612 --> 00:01:11,088
You've seen the films.

28
00:01:11,088 --> 00:01:12,436
Something jumps out.

29
00:01:12,436 --> 00:01:15,708
But that's not the thing that actually got people.

30
00:01:15,708 --> 00:01:19,045
The dangerous part of the night wasn't the monster.

31
00:01:19,045 --> 00:01:21,163
It was this: they could not see.

32
00:01:21,163 --> 00:01:24,179
And anything that could see better than them —

33
00:01:24,179 --> 00:01:27,965
anything with better ears, better eyes, better instincts —

34
00:01:27,965 --> 00:01:29,185
had the advantage.

35
00:01:29,185 --> 00:01:31,559
So they did the only sensible thing.

36
00:01:31,559 --> 00:01:35,345
They went to the one place where the dark stopped working.

37
00:01:35,345 --> 00:01:39,837
There's a small fire in a hollow by the water, and it's not dramatic.

38
00:01:39,837 --> 00:01:41,506
It's not a wall of flame.

39
00:01:41,506 --> 00:01:44,458
It's a pile of dry wood and a few hot stones,

40
00:01:44,458 --> 00:01:48,822
kept alive by people who have done this every night of their lives.

41
00:01:48,822 --> 00:01:50,490
But look at what it does.

42
00:01:50,490 --> 00:01:53,173
It pushes the dark back about ten paces.

43
00:01:53,173 --> 00:01:56,444
Inside those ten paces, things have colour again.

44
00:01:56,444 --> 00:01:56,902
Faces.

45
00:01:56,902 --> 00:01:57,360
Hands.

46
00:01:57,360 --> 00:01:58,015
The fish.

47
00:01:58,015 --> 00:02:00,305
The shape of your kid's shoulders.

48
00:02:00,305 --> 00:02:05,146
Outside those ten paces, the dark is still exactly where it was, waiting,

49
00:02:05,146 --> 00:02:07,633
and everyone in this family knows it.

50
00:02:07,633 --> 00:02:09,465
Fire didn't beat the night.

51
00:02:09,465 --> 00:02:12,802
Fire bought them a small room in the middle of it.

52
00:02:12,802 --> 00:02:14,961
And a small room is worth a lot.

53
00:02:14,961 --> 00:02:19,737
It's the difference between sitting in the dark listening for something,

54
00:02:19,737 --> 00:02:23,074
and sitting in the light telling stories about it.

55
00:02:23,074 --> 00:02:26,150
Here's the part of this that actually matters.

56
00:02:26,150 --> 00:02:28,440
A fire doesn't stay lit by itself.

57
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:30,075
Somebody has to feed it.

58
00:02:30,075 --> 00:02:34,917
Somebody has to sit there, awake, while everyone else sleeps, and listen.

59
00:02:34,917 --> 00:02:37,469
And then somebody has to relieve them,

60
00:02:37,469 --> 00:02:42,704
because a person cannot do that all night and still be a person in the morning.

61
00:02:42,704 --> 00:02:44,012
So they took turns.

62
00:02:44,012 --> 00:02:46,171
Think about how strange that is.

63
00:02:46,171 --> 00:02:50,948
This is the first job in human history that has nothing to do with food.

64
00:02:50,948 --> 00:02:52,322
It produces nothing.

65
00:02:52,322 --> 00:02:55,004
You can't eat it, carry it, or trade it.

66
00:02:55,004 --> 00:02:59,650
All it produces is the fact that other people get to close their eyes.

67
00:02:59,650 --> 00:03:03,735
Renn volunteers for the first watch, because of course he does.

68
00:03:03,735 --> 00:03:05,075
He sits up straight.

69
00:03:05,075 --> 00:03:07,372
He puts the spear across his knees.

70
00:03:07,372 --> 00:03:12,797
He looks out at the dark with the expression of a man who is extremely good at this.

71
00:03:12,797 --> 00:03:14,712
He lasts about forty minutes.

72
00:03:14,712 --> 00:03:17,328
Sena takes over without saying anything,

73
00:03:17,328 --> 00:03:20,711
which is somehow worse than if she'd said something.

74
00:03:20,711 --> 00:03:21,796
Tep won't sleep.

75
00:03:21,796 --> 00:03:23,008
Tep has questions.

76
00:03:23,008 --> 00:03:24,668
Tep always has questions.

77
00:03:24,668 --> 00:03:26,965
"Why is the dark scary?" And Sena —

78
00:03:26,965 --> 00:03:30,412
who is tired, and who has spent all day gutting fish,

79
00:03:30,412 --> 00:03:33,220
and who is not in the mood for philosophy —

80
00:03:33,220 --> 00:03:36,921
gives the answer that people have been giving ever since.

81
00:03:36,921 --> 00:03:40,751
"Because we can't see what's in it." Tep thinks about this.

82
00:03:40,751 --> 00:03:43,814
Then Tep says the thing that changes the night.

83
00:03:43,814 --> 00:03:46,494
"So tell me what's in it." And that's it.

84
00:03:46,494 --> 00:03:47,898
That's the beginning.

85
00:03:47,898 --> 00:03:51,600
Right there, by the fire, two hundred thousand years ago,

86
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:55,110
a kid asks for a story so the dark stops being empty —

87
00:03:55,110 --> 00:03:56,706
and the adult tells one.

88
00:03:56,706 --> 00:03:57,727
Not a true one.

89
00:03:57,727 --> 00:03:58,620
Not a lesson.

90
00:03:58,620 --> 00:03:59,195
A story.

91
00:03:59,195 --> 00:04:05,130
Something with a shape to it, that ends, that you can hold in your head instead of the dark.

92
00:04:05,130 --> 00:04:08,974
She tells him about a man who followed a deer for three days.

93
00:04:08,974 --> 00:04:11,392
She makes the deer bigger than it was.

94
00:04:11,392 --> 00:04:13,128
She makes the man stupider.

95
00:04:13,128 --> 00:04:18,522
Tep laughs, and then Tep is asleep, and Sena is still talking for a minute after that,

96
00:04:18,522 --> 00:04:21,499
because it's easier to keep going than to stop.

97
00:04:21,499 --> 00:04:24,041
So what did they actually do after dark?

98
00:04:24,041 --> 00:04:25,281
They mended things.

99
00:04:25,281 --> 00:04:26,397
They made things.

100
00:04:26,397 --> 00:04:27,017
They ate.

101
00:04:27,017 --> 00:04:29,745
They talked, properly, for the first time —

102
00:04:29,745 --> 00:04:31,481
not shouting across a hunt,

103
00:04:31,481 --> 00:04:31,671
but

104
00:04:31,671 --> 00:04:37,619
sitting close and saying the things you only say when your hands are busy and the day is over.

105
00:04:37,619 --> 00:04:39,851
They told each other what happened.

106
00:04:39,851 --> 00:04:42,703
And because nobody was writing anything down,

107
00:04:42,703 --> 00:04:45,679
the telling was the only way anything got kept.

108
00:04:45,679 --> 00:04:49,275
The night is where memory started being a group activity.

109
00:04:49,275 --> 00:04:51,384
And they looked after each other.

110
00:04:51,384 --> 00:04:54,608
Somebody fed the fire so somebody else could sleep.

111
00:04:54,608 --> 00:04:55,290
That's it.

112
00:04:55,290 --> 00:04:56,778
That's the whole thing.

113
00:04:56,778 --> 00:04:59,134
It's not a temple, it's not a ritual,

114
00:04:59,134 --> 00:05:03,164
it's a person sitting up so another person can close their eyes.

115
00:05:03,164 --> 00:05:05,210
It's still three in the morning.

116
00:05:05,210 --> 00:05:06,938
The phone is still there.

117
00:05:06,938 --> 00:05:10,461
The conversation from four years ago is still there.

118
00:05:10,461 --> 00:05:15,313
But somewhere under all of that, there's something much older going on —

119
00:05:15,313 --> 00:05:18,304
a system that decided, a very long time ago,

120
00:05:18,304 --> 00:05:22,823
that being awake when everyone else is asleep is a job worth doing.

121
00:05:22,823 --> 00:05:24,551
Someone did that for you.

122
00:05:24,551 --> 00:05:28,805
For thousands of years, someone sat up so that you could sleep.

123
00:05:28,805 --> 00:05:30,467
Tonight, it's your turn.

124
00:05:30,467 --> 00:05:31,929
You're the one awake.

125
00:05:31,929 --> 00:05:33,192
So check the fire.

126
00:05:33,192 --> 00:05:34,056
Listen once.

127
00:05:34,056 --> 00:05:34,787
And then —

128
00:05:34,787 --> 00:05:40,304
like Sena, like every tired person who ever kept a small light going in the dark —

129
00:05:40,304 --> 00:05:41,168
go to sleep.

130
00:05:41,168 --> 00:05:43,494
Someone's covering the next watch.

131
00:05:43,494 --> 00:05:43,760
---
